rdiovascular risks.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Alpha-blockers and other agents require blood pressure monitoring\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Drugs such as 盐酸特拉唑嗪 (terazosin hydrochloride) and 盐酸普拉克索 (pramipexole hydrochloride) are associated with blood pressure effects that necessitate clinical vigilance. Terazosin, an alpha-1 blocker, requires blood pressure monitoring during use due to its hypotensive potential [Data: Relationships (3583); Entities (7286)]. Similarly, pramipexole, a dopamine agonist used in Parkinson’s disease, warrants blood pressure checks, especially early in treatment [Data: Relationships (3561); Entities (7254)]. These examples illustrate that blood pressure monitoring extends beyond cardiovascular drugs to include agents from diverse therapeutic classes.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Weak vision is a documented adverse reaction to certain medications\",\n            \"explanation\": \"弱视 (amblyopia or visual impairment) is listed as a systemic adverse effect associated with 地高辛 (digoxin), particularly in cases of toxicity. Evidence indicates that digoxin’s narrow therapeutic index can lead to serious side effects including nausea, confusion, weak vision, dry eyes, and life-threatening arrhythmias [Data: Relationships (10579); Entities (15920)]. Although digoxin itself is not among the primary entities listed in the input entity table, the relationship record explicitly ties it to 弱视, confirming a documented drug-side effect link within this data ecosystem.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Injectable hepatoprotective agents may influence blood pressure\",\n            \"explanation\": \"注射用甘草酸二铵 (diammonium glycyrrhizinate injection), used for liver protection, is associated with potential blood pressure effects, possibly due to its mineralocorticoid-like activity leading to sodium retention and hypertension [Data: Relationships (2850); Entities (6515)]. This aligns w